        "id": "maxim:photography:history-arc",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "photography",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[history, arc]",
        "section": "photography",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "History Arc",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "1826  Niépce: 8-hour exposure on pewter — first photograph\n  1839  Daguerre: daguerreotype publicly announced (August 19 — World Photography Day)\n  1839  Talbot: calotype (negative-positive) announced; different paradigm\n  1851  Archer: wet collodion — 10-second exposures; revolution in speed\n  1861  Maxwell: first color photograph (additive projection, tartan ribbon)\n  1871  Maddox: gelatin dry plate — freed from darkroom tent\n  1884  Eastman: flexible roll film\n  1888  Kodak #1: roll film; mail-in processing; \"You press the button\"\n  1900  Kodak Brownie: $1 camera; mass photography begins\n  1907  Lumière Autochrome: first commercial color process\n  1913  Leica prototype (Barnack): 35mm cine film in still camera\n  1925  Leica Model I: commercial 35mm; handheld documentary photography\n  1932  Ansel Adams: f/64 Group; straight photography; zone system\n  1935  Kodachrome: first modern color film (3-layer; K-14 process)\n  1936  35mm SLR (Exakta) precursor; 1954: Asahi Pentax — modern 35mm SLR\n  1947  Land: Polaroid instant photography\n  1948  Hasselblad 1600F: Swedish medium format SLR\n  1963  Instamatic: Kodak 126 cartridge; easier loading\n  1975  Sasson (Kodak): first digital camera prototype (0.01 MP, 23 seconds)\n  1985  Minolta Maxxum 7000: first production autofocus SLR\n  1991  Kodak DCS-100: first commercial digital SLR\n  2000  Sharp J-SH04 (Japan): first camera phone\n  2007  iPhone: capacitive touch; real UI; begins smartphone camera era\n  2010  Instagram: social distribution defines the purpose of photography\n  2016  Google Night Sight + Pixel: computational photography mainstream\n  2022+ AI generation: Stable Diffusion, DALL-E, Midjourney — image synthesis without camera\n```"
    },
